  "meta_story": {
    "id": "computing_pioneers",
    "title": "Computing’s First Architects",
    "tagline": "From engines to software empires",
    "description": "The story of computing isn’t a straight line from one “great invention” to another—it’s a relay race across generations. It begins with a poet-mathematician imagining a machine that manipulates symbols, not just numbers; it accelerates through wartime urgency that turned abstract logic into working electronic systems; and it matures as programming becomes a discipline, not a craft, enabling computers to scale from room-sized prototypes to everyday tools.\n\nThis meta-story follows pioneers who each solved a different bottleneck: how to describe computation in theory, how to build reliable automatic machines, how to make those machines usable through languages and compilers, how to reason about correctness and efficiency, and how to bring computing into institutions and homes. Along the way, a critical voice emerges to warn that power without reflection can become a new kind of trap—especially as software begins to imitate conversation itself.",
    "person_ids": [
      "ada_lovelace",
      "alan_turing",
      "john_von_neumann",
      "konrad_zuse",
      "grace_hopper",
      "edsger_w_dijkstra",
